  {
   "id": 2,
   "ref": "90–95",
   "en": "[As soon as|2,3] Jupiter's own dear wife saw that Dido was gripped by such a sickness, and that concern for her reputation could no longer stand in the way of her passion, Saturn's daughter went to Venus with these words: 'What splendid glory, what rich spoils, you two are winning — you and that boy of yours, quite the great and memorable pair of gods — if it takes two of you to [overpower|39,41] one mere woman!",
   "en_marked": "[As soon as|2,3] [Jupiter's|9] {own|Not in the Latin. Added for emphasis, bringing out Juno's status in cara Iovis coniunx.} [dear|8] [wife|10] [saw|5] that [Dido|1] [was gripped|7] by [such|4] a [sickness|6], [and|11] that {concern for|Added for clarity: the Latin says only that her reputation (famam) no longer holds back her passion.} her [reputation|12] could [no longer|11] [stand in the way of|13] her [passion|14], [Saturn's daughter|18] [went to|16] [Venus|17] with [these|15] [words|19]: 'What [splendid|20] [glory|22], what [rich|25] [spoils|24], [you two are winning|26] — [you|27] [and|28] that [boy|28] [of yours|29], {quite the|Not in the Latin. Added to bring out Juno's sarcasm in magnum et memorabile numen.} [great|30] [and|31] [memorable|32] [pair of gods|33] — [if|37] it takes [two|40] [of you|36] to [overpower|39,41] [one|34] {mere|Not in the Latin. Added to sharpen Juno's mockery: two gods against one woman.} [woman|38]!",
